# Versioning, ownership, updates

This document has been created as a guide to versioning, ownership and updates for software development teams within the Ministry of Justice (**MOJ**) and includes guidance and best practice.

Software development can best be described as a “journey” rather than a “destination”. The team needs to be able to update code to ensure it continues to function correctly.

GitHub has resources to help you manage updates, and links are included in the document.

## Versioning

**Versioning** is a process used by software development teams to keep track of any changes made to software.

The unique state of the software at a specific point in time is called a **version**.

The **version** number is the unique identifier for each version. Version numbers record when specific changes were made, which helps when performing a rollback to a previous version if the software doesn’t function as it should.

Development at MOJ uses a semantic versioning system with a string in three parts - major, minor and patch. When an update is completed the relevant part of the string is incremented by 1.

**major**.**minor**.**patch**

Major updates introduce a significant change in software architecture, functionality or both.
These new features often break backward compatibility, where previous code versions no longer function without the change being deployed.

**1.0.0	> 2.0.0**

Minor updates add new features or improve existing features without breaking backward compatibility or changing the software architecture.

**2.0.0 > 2.1.0**

Patches are minor changes which fix bugs, security vulnerabilities or other issues. There are no changes to software features or functionality.

**2.1.0 > 2.1.1**

**Versioning best practice**

- avoid unnecessary breaking changes in minor/patch releases
- reserve major version deployments for breaking changes
- deprecate versions gradually, and inform consumers when necessary
- provide migration guides to help consumers understand changes (when necessary) and update their code

To create a point of reference for each version, you can add **tags**. Tags should include annotations with information to verify the change.

GitHub documentation contains more details on [working with releases](https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/releasing-projects-on-github/about-releases), [viewing releases and tags in a repository](https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/releasing-projects-on-github/viewing-your-repositorys-releases-and-tags) and [signing tags](https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/managing-commit-signature-verification/signing-tags).

Keep a full summary of each version’s changes in a **changelog**. GitHub doesn’t have a specific changelog format, although [release](https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/releasing-projects-on-github/about-releases) functions can capture version history and a [commit history](https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/viewing-activity-and-data-for-your-repository/viewing-commit-history) is also available.

**Changelog best practice**

- maintain a CHANGELOG.md file in the repository
- use GitHub releases to help populate the information for each version

## Ownership

GitHub has a hierarchy of roles to help define repository access for users within an organisation. If a user does not need to write code for a repository, for example, they can be granted read-only access.

Full information on [managing repository roles](https://docs.github.com/en/organizations/managing-user-access-to-your-organizations-repositories/managing-repository-roles/repository-roles-for-an-organization) is available in GitHub’s online documentation.

An owner is an [individual user](https://docs.github.com/en/organizations/managing-user-access-to-your-organizations-repositories/managing-repository-roles/managing-an-individuals-access-to-an-organization-repository) (or [team](https://docs.github.com/en/organizations/managing-user-access-to-your-organizations-repositories/managing-repository-roles/managing-team-access-to-an-organization-repository)) responsible for managing the code in a repository.

**Ownership best practice**

- create and use a [CODEOWNERS.md](https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/managing-your-repositorys-settings-and-features/customizing-your-repository/about-code-owners) file in the repository
- document ownership in further locations if it’s required (README.md file, architectural decision records)

## Updates

Managing software updates is a critical process in the development lifecycle. Dependencies and vulnerabilities can appear over time in code which has not been adapted to accommodate changes (operational or technical).

**Update management best practice**

- develop incrementally and continuously (updates deployed and integrated when ready)
- avoid deploying multiple updates at once
- confirm a priority level, as some updates (security fixes) will have greater importance

To help manage code updates in GitHub, [Dependabot](https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/code-security/tutorials/secure-your-dependencies/dependabot-quickstart) checks for any conflict in dependencies and security vulnerabilities, generating automatic pull requests for any updates required by deploying the new version. The [MOJ developer portal](https://developer-portal.service.justice.gov.uk/) contains guidance on [using Dependabot in MOJ repositories](https://developer-portal.service.justice.gov.uk/guidelines/dependabot-guidelines).

## GitHub Actions

[GitHub Actions](https://docs.github.com/en/actions) documentation contains instructions on supporting versioning and updates, including

- automated update management
- scanning updates for vulnerabilities
- using a CI/CD pipeline for testing and deploying updates

**GitHub Actions best practice**

When full Secure Hash Algorithm (**SHA**) commits are pinned to the version of each action, updates are defined according to the expected version. This secures against supply chain attacks by preventing malware from entering the system.
